T*
(PAGE 96: SLEEP APNEA \(OBSTRUCTIVE\) AND NOCTURNAL HYPOXEMIA) Tj
T*
(Section: Sleep Medicine, Pulmonology & Upper Airway Physiology) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Definition and Clinical Overview: Obstructive sleep apnea \(OSA\) is characterized) Tj
T*
(by repetitive episodes of complete \(apnea\) or partial \(hypopnea\) upper airway) Tj
T*
(collapse during sleep.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(What Is the Reason \(Underlying Etiology & Mechanisms\): Pharyngeal muscle) Tj
T*
(relaxation during REM and deep sleep allows the tongue and soft palate to obstruct) Tj
T*
(the airway, causing episodic hypoxemia and cortical arousals.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Immediate Action Plan \(What to Do Right Now for Relief\): Sleep on your side rather) Tj
T*
(than your back \(positional therapy\), avoid alcohol and sedatives before bed, and) Tj
T*
(elevate the head of the bed.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Proper Plan: How to Cure & Prevent \(Long-Term Management\): Undergo a) Tj
T*
(polysomnography sleep study; first-line therapy is nocturnal Continuous Positive) Tj
T*
(Airway Pressure \(CPAP\), weight management, or oral mandibular advancement splints.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Red-Flag Warning Signs \(When to Seek Emergency Care\): Seek medical evaluation for) Tj
T*
(severe daytime sleepiness, falling asleep while driving, witnessed nocturnal) Tj
T*
(choking/gasping, or uncontrolled morning hypertension.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
